### Mini Gemini Code Guide: **C++ Memory Management & Smart Pointers**

C++ delivers high-performance, low-level control over hardware resources. Modern C++ standardizes safety with smart pointer constructs.

#### 1. Memory Management Example (C++17)
Here is a class using modern smart pointers to manage dynamic object allocations:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <vector>
#include <string>

class SkillNode {
public:
    std::string name;
    SkillNode(std::string n) : name(std::move(n)) {
        std::cout << "Skill Node Created: " << name << "\n";
    }
    ~SkillNode() {
        std::cout << "Skill Node Destroyed: " << name << "\n";
    }
};

class UserPortfolio {
private:
    // std::unique_ptr ensures single ownership scope
    std::unique_ptr<SkillNode> primarySkill;
    // std::shared_ptr enables shared ownership
    std::vector<std::shared_ptr<SkillNode>> projectSkills;

public:
    void setPrimarySkill(std::string name) {
        primarySkill = std::make_unique<SkillNode>(name);
    }

    void addProjectSkill(const std::shared_ptr<SkillNode>& node) {
        projectSkills.push_back(node);
    }
};

int main() {
    UserPortfolio portfolio;
    portfolio.setPrimarySkill("C++ Template Metaprogramming");

    auto sharedNode = std::make_shared<SkillNode>("Embedded Linux");
    portfolio.addProjectSkill(sharedNode);
    // Destructors trigger automatically when scope closes, avoiding memory leaks.
    return 0;
}
```

#### 2. C++ Guidelines:
*   **Avoid Raw Pointers:** Never use `new` or `delete` directly; manage memory using `std::unique_ptr` and `std::shared_ptr`.
*   **RAII:** Resource Acquisition Is Initialization. Keep socket, file, and memory lifespans tied to stack lifetimes.

